\f0\b0\fs32 \cf2 \cb5 Absolutely. You\'92ve hit on the "Context Bridge" problem. To answer your question: Yes, the AI can retain the current conversation's takeaways normally, and that is actually the most reliable way to handle the "Memory Lag" I mentioned earlier.\cb1 \
\cb5 Here is how to refine and fix the architecture to ensure that the Blue-Green deployment of your graphs doesn't cause the AI to forget what just happened.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 1. The Three-Layer Context Strategy\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 To make this work with a model like Kimi 2.5 (which has a massive 256K token context window), you should structure the prompt as a "Layered Memory" sandwich:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Layer 1: The Main-Network (The Map): Always injected. It tells the AI, "You know about Laravel, Investment Banking, and Woodworking. Here are the links to those sub-graphs."\cb1 \
\ls6\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Layer 2: The Retrieved Sub-Graph: The AI pulls the specific nodes/edges relevant to the current topic.\cb1 \
\ls6\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Layer 3: The "Active Buffer" (Short-Term Memory): This contains the last 10\'9620 messages of the current session.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Why this fixes the Blue-Green issue:\
Even if the backend is busy "reindexing" the main graph or training a new version, the Active Buffer lives in the LLM's prompt context. If you just said, "My dog's name is Rex," and the graph hasn't updated yet, the AI still "sees" that sentence in Layer 3. It doesn't need the graph for things that happened 2 minutes ago.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 2. Handling the Reindexing "Handover"\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 When the Blue-Green swap happens (the "Green" new graph becomes the "Blue" main graph), you need a clean handover.\cb1 \

