\ls16\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Duplicate Entities: "David," "David Echu," and "The developer" being treated as three separate nodes.\cb1 \
\ls16\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Low-Value Triples: Storing fluff like 
\fs28 \cf4 \cb3 (User, Says, "Hello")
\fs32 \cf2 \cb5 .\cb1 \
\ls16\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Property Bloat: Attaching massive chunks of raw text as "metadata" to every single node.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 2. The Solution: "Deduction, Not Transcription"\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 You are exactly right\'97the AI should only create nodes from Deductions.\cb1 \
\cb5 Instead of a "log everything" approach, your Rust middleware should enforce a Triple Reflection step. Before a new piece of data is "async-trained" into the Green graph, the AI must pass it through a "Gatekeeper" prompt (using Kimi's Thinking Mode):\cb1 \
\cb5 Gatekeeper Prompt: "Based on the last 5 messages, what are the permanent, factual changes to the state of our project? Ignore conversational filler. Output only as structured triples."\cb1 \
