\cf2 \cb5 2. Implementing the "Confidence Score" in Rust\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 The Confidence Score isn't a "feeling"; it\'92s a mathematical or logical weight attached to an edge (relationship) in your graph. Since you are using Rust, you can add a 
\fs28 \cf4 \cb3 weight: f32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb5  or 
\fs28 \cf4 \cb3 confidence: u8
\fs32 \cf2 \cb5  field to your Graph Data Structure.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 How the Reviewer Calculates the Score:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 The Reviewer model should look for three specific "Signals of Truth":\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\ls23\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Explicit Confirmation: User said "Yes, that's right" or "Correct." (
\fs38\fsmilli19360 +40
\fs32  points)\cb1 \
\ls23\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Repetition: The same fact has been mentioned in 3 different messages. (
\fs38\fsmilli19360 +30
\fs32  points)\cb1 \
\ls23\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Logical Consistency: The new fact doesn't contradict existing nodes. (
\fs38\fsmilli19360 +20
\fs32  points)\cb1 \
\ls23\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Model Certainty: The LLM uses high-certainty language ("X is Y" vs. "X might be Y"). (
\fs38\fsmilli19360 +10
\fs32  points)\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 The "Promotion" Logic:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\ls24\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Score < 50: Keep in a "Buffer/Draft" table. (The AI can see it but shouldn't treat it as "Gospel").\cb1 \
\ls24\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Score 50-80: Add to the Sub-Graph but mark it as 
\fs28 \cf4 \cb3 unverified
\fs32 \cf2 \cb5 .\cb1 \
\ls24\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Score > 80: Permanent "Gold" status. Update the Main Network to reflect this new solid knowledge.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 3. The "Stateful Handover" in Rust\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 To keep this "light" on your MacBook's RAM, you don't need three separate LLM instances running. You can use the same Ollama/Kimi session but change the System Prompt for each step.\cb1 \
\cb5 The Workflow:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\ls25\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Researcher Step: Send prompt to Kimi: "You are the Researcher. Based on this user query, what tools do you need to call?"\cb1 \
\ls25\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Rust Logic: Execute the tool calls and get the data.\cb1 \
\ls25\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Actor Step: Send data + prompt to Kimi: "You are the Actor. Use this data to answer the user."\cb1 \
\ls25\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Reviewer Step (Async): Send the whole chat to Kimi: "You are the Reviewer. What was the 1 most important deduction? Give me a confidence score 1-100."\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 4. Why 3 is "The Magic Number"\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\ls26\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If you had 2: You lack a "neutral" third party to verify if the Actor ignored the Researcher's data.\cb1 \
\ls26\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If you had 4+: You'd hit "diminishing returns" where the latency (wait time) becomes too high for a natural conversation, and your 16GB RAM would start to struggle with the overhead.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Technical Tip for Rust Developers:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Use a Crate like 
\fs28 \cf4 \cb3 petgraph
\fs32 \cf2 \cb5  for your local graph operations. It allows you to attach "Weights" (your Confidence Scores) to edges easily.\cb1 \
\cb5 When the Reviewer decides a score has dropped (e.g., the user said "Actually, I'm not using MySQL anymore"), Rust can instantly prune that edge or lower its weight so the Researcher stops finding it.\cb1 \
\cb5 One final thought: To keep the "3-man" operation fast, have the Reviewer run completely in the background (asynchronously). The user gets their answer from the Actor, and while they are reading it, the Reviewer is silently updating the graph for the next turn.\cb1 \
\cb5 Does the Reviewer have a "Delete" permission in your mind, or should it only ever add/update info?\cb1 \
