\f0\b0\fs32 \cf2 \cb5 This architecture is becoming a robust, self-correcting memory engine. By restricting Write access to the Reviewer while allowing Read access to all three, you prevent "hallucination loops" where the Actor might accidentally invent and then immediately save a false fact.\cb1 \
\cb5 Here is how to structure the data access and the Reviewer\'92s logic in your Rust implementation.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 1. The "Read-Only" Shared State\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 In Rust, you can use an 
\fs28 \cf4 \cb3 Arc<RwLock<KnowledgeGraph>>
\fs32 \cf2 \cb5  (Atomic Reference Counter with a Read-Write Lock). This allows the Researcher, Actor, and Reviewer to all read from the graph simultaneously without blocking each other.\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\ls27\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The Researcher: Reads to find context.\cb1 \
\ls27\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The Actor: Reads to verify its response doesn't contradict the project roadmap.\cb1 \
\ls27\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The Reviewer: Reads to compare new deductions against old ones before hitting "Save."\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 2. Multi-Deduction & Cross-Verification\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 The Reviewer shouldn't just "dump" data. It should perform a Conflict Check.\cb1 \
\cb5 The Reviewer\'92s Workflow:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\ls28\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Extract: Finds multiple potential deductions (e.g., "User switched to PostgreSQL," "Deadline moved to Friday").\cb1 \
\ls28\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Verify: It calls a 
\fs28 \cf4 \cb3 check_conflict
\fs32 \cf2 \cb5  tool on the graph.\cb1 \
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\sa200\partightenfactor0
\ls28\ilvl1\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Example: If the graph says 
\fs28 \cf4 \cb3 (Database, Is, "MySQL")
\fs32 \cf2 \cb5  and the new deduction is 
\fs28 \cf4 \cb3 (Database, Is, "PostgreSQL")
\fs32 \cf2 \cb5 , the Reviewer sees a conflict.\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\ls28\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Resolve: It doesn't just add a new node. It calculates: "The user explicitly said 'switched,' so I will update the existing node and lower the confidence of the MySQL node."\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 3. Handling the "Draft" vs. "Sub-Graph" Access\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 To keep the system "smart" but "safe," you should treat your graph queries like a tiered search. When any of the 3 modes (Researcher, Actor, or Reviewer) calls a tool to "find knowledge," the Rust backend should return results from both:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\ls29\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Tier 1 (The Gold Standard): High-confidence nodes (Score > 80).\cb1 \
\ls29\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Tier 2 (The Drafts): Recent deductions with lower confidence (Score < 50).\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Why the Actor needs Tier 2: If you just told the AI "My name is David" in the last message, and the Reviewer has only put it in "Drafts" with a 40% score, the Actor still needs to see it so it can say "Hello, David." If it only looked at "Gold" data, it would feel like the AI has a 5-minute memory lag.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 4. The Reviewer as the "Librarian"\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Since only the Reviewer can Update or Create, it acts as the "Gatekeeper."\cb1 \

