\f0\b0\fs32 \cf2 \cb5 This is a very practical way to approach the build. In fact, separating the Graph Management System from the LLM is not only possible\'97it\'92s a superior engineering pattern. It allows you to build a "Deterministic Core" that you can verify with unit tests before adding the "Probabilistic" LLM on top.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 1. Can you split them?\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Yes. You can build the entire graph engine as a standalone CLI tool or library in Rust.\cb1 \
\cb5 In this setup, the "3-man operation" becomes a set of API endpoints or CLI commands that a human can trigger manually to test the logic. Once it works for a human, it will work for an LLM.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 2. The "Interface-First" Architecture\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 To make this work, your Rust package should expose a Tool Definition Schema (like a JSON file or an MCP - Model Context Protocol server).\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\ls36\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Phase 1 (The Engine): You build the Rust CLI. You test it by typing commands like 
\fs28 \cf4 \cb3 kg-manage add-node --label "Rust" --props '\{"type": "language"\}'
\fs32 \cf2 \cb5 .\cb1 \
\ls36\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Phase 2 (The LLM Bridge): You point an LLM (like Ollama or Kimi) to your package. You tell the LLM: "Here is a CLI tool. Use these commands to store what we talk about."\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 3. File & Folder View (Standalone Package)\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Since you want this to be a 
\fs28 \cf4 \cb3 brew
\fs32 \cf2 \cb5  installable package, you should follow the standard Rust binary structure:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf4 \cb9 Plaintext\cb1 \
\
\cb9 rust-kg-core/\

\f4 \'a9\'c0
\f0 \uc0\u9472 \u9472  Cargo.toml\

\f4 \'a9\'c0
\f0 \uc0\u9472 \u9472  brew/                       # Formula for Homebrew distribution\
\uc0\u9474    \u9492 \u9472 \u9472  rust-kg.rb\

\f4 \'a9\'c0
\f0 \uc0\u9472 \u9472  src/\
\uc0\u9474    
\f4 \'a9\'c0
\f0 \uc0\u9472 \u9472  main.rs                 # CLI Entry point (clap-rs for arguments)\
\uc0\u9474    
\f4 \'a9\'c0
\f0 \uc0\u9472 \u9472  lib.rs                  # The core logic as a library\
\uc0\u9474    
\f4 \'a9\'c0
\f0 \uc0\u9472 \u9472  storage/                # Where the actual .db or JSON files live\
\uc0\u9474    
\f4 \'a9\'c0
\f0 \uc0\u9472 \u9472  schema/                 # Rules: What nodes/edges are allowed\
\uc0\u9474    \u9492 \u9472 \u9472  api/                    # JSON-RPC or MCP server (for LLM to connect)\

\f4 \'a9\'c0
\f0 \uc0\u9472 \u9472  examples/                   # Example graph configs\
\uc0\u9492 \u9472 \u9472  tests/                      # Testing the "Blue-Green" swap without an AI\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf6 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 4. How to Test Without an LLM\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 You can "Mock" the LLM's role. Write a small shell script that simulates a conversation and calls your Rust binary:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\ls37\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Script: Calls 
\fs28 \cf4 \cb3 kg-manage extract --text "David is building a Rust app."
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
\ls37\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Rust Engine: Performs the "Deduction" (using a hardcoded regex or a tiny local model) and creates a "Draft."\cb1 \
\ls37\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Script: Calls 
\fs28 \cf4 \cb3 kg-manage review --confirm-all
\fs32 \cf2 \cb5 .\cb1 \
\ls37\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Rust Engine: Performs the "Blue-Green" swap and moves data to the "Gold" sub-graph.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 5. Why GitHub + Brew is a Good Idea\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\ls38\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Version Control: You can version your Graph Schema just like code.\cb1 \
\ls38\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Portability: By making it a 
\fs28 \cf4 \cb3 brew
\fs32 \cf2 \cb5  package, you can easily install it on your MacBook and other machines, ensuring the environment is consistent.\cb1 \
\ls38\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
MCP Support: If you build this as an MCP Server (Model Context Protocol), tools like the Zed Editor (which you already use) or Claude Desktop can connect to your graph instantly without you writing any extra "connector" code.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Strategy Recommendation:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Start by building the Storage and Reviewer logic in Rust first. Use a simple JSON or CSV input to simulate "AI deductions." Once your Rust code can handle 1,000 nodes, resolve conflicts, and manage confidence scores without breaking, then\'97and only then\'97connect the Ollama/Kimi API.\cb1 \
\cb5 If you go this route, I'd suggest looking into {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.google.com/search?q=https://www.youtube.com/watch%3Fv%3DXid7899P_pA"}}{\fldrslt \cf8 \ul \ulc8 The Model Context Protocol (MCP)}}. It's the industry standard for connecting local tools to LLMs, and there are excellent Rust crates to get you started.\cb1 \
\cb5 This video explains how the Model Context Protocol allows you to connect local data sources and tools directly to AI models, which perfectly fits your plan of building a standalone package that an LLM can later "plug into."\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

